{"name":"Salamander","hex":"#63775b","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"salamander","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A long, slender, chiefly terrestrial amphibian of the order Caudata, superficially resembling a lizard.","A creature much like a lizard that is resistant to and lives in fire (in which it is often depicted in heraldry), hence the elemental being of fire."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To use a salamander (cooking utensil) in a cooking process."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/salamander"}],"entries":[{"title":"Salamander","blocks":[{"text":"Salamanders are a group of amphibians typically characterized by their lizard-like appearance, with slender bodies, blunt snouts, short limbs projecting at right angles to the body, and the presence of a tail in both larvae and adults. All ten extant salamander families are grouped together under the order Urodela, the sole surviving order from the group Caudata. Urodela is a scientific Latin term based on the Ancient Greek οὐρά δήλη, \"conspicuous tail\". Caudata is the Latin for \"tailed ones\", from cauda, \"tail\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/SpottedSalamander.jpg/330px-SpottedSalamander.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salamander","wikidata":"Q53663"},{"title":"Salamander (video game)","blocks":[{"text":"Salamander, released as Life Force in North America and in the Japanese arcade re-release, is a 1986 scrolling shooter video game developed and published by Konami for arcades. A spin-off of the Gradius series, Salamander introduced a simplified power-up system, two-player cooperative gameplay and both horizontally and vertically scrolling stages. Some of these later became normal for future Gradius games. In Japanese, the title is written using ateji, which are kanji used for spelling foreign words that has been supplanted in everyday use by katakana. Contra, another game by Konami was also given this treatment, with its title written in Japanese as 魂斗羅."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/1/11/Salamander_flyer.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salamander_(video_game)","wikidata":"Q1370988"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Salamandra_lanzai13.jpg/500px-Salamandra_lanzai13.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Salamandra_lanzai13.jpg/500px-Salamandra_lanzai13.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"X-ray image of salamander","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASalamandra_lanzai13.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/S_lacertina_USGS.jpg/500px-S_lacertina_USGS.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/S_lacertina_USGS.jpg/500px-S_lacertina_USGS.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sirens have an eel-like appearance.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AS_lacertina_USGS.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Taricha_granulosa_%28Rough-skinned_newt%29.JPG/500px-Taricha_granulosa_%28Rough-skinned_newt%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Taricha_granulosa_%28Rough-skinned_newt%29.JPG/1280px-Taricha_granulosa_%28Rough-skinned_newt%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Taricha_granulosa_%28Rough-skinned_newt%29.JPG/500px-Taricha_granulosa_%28Rough-skinned_newt%29.JPG?utm_source=en.w